+RDHC_55=The router's resolvers answer reverse lookups (address to name) between the main LAN and every VLAN, in both directions, so a DNS filter on a VLAN such as AdGuard shows main-LAN devices by name. Guest and Portal networks are never included, and VLANs are not shared with each other. Turn this off to keep each network's device names to itself.
+RDHC_56=IPv6 device names
+RDHC_57=Name devices by their IPv6 addresses too
+RDHC_58=Devices usually ask DNS over IPv6 from addresses the router never handed out, so a DNS filter such as AdGuard shows them without a name. The router matches each IPv6 address to the device that uses it and answers with the name from its IPv4 lease, for reverse and forward lookups, refreshed every minute. Works only while IPv6 is enabled on the router.
 RVR_64=Rules choose the path. Active - Killswitch means that client's Killswitch is on: if its tunnel drops, this traffic is blocked rather than sent out the WAN. Inactive - WAN means that client is switched off, so this traffic uses the WAN. Both are set on that VPN client's own page.
 RVR_65=LAN
 RVR_66=WireGuard targets need the same hardware-acceleration bypass VPN Director uses, for every rule that names one. A source rule bypasses only that address; a destination or MAC rule bypasses the whole LAN, so LAN traffic loses hardware acceleration while such a rule exists.
